course: language
lesson: 13
title: Silent letters and the mystery of -ough
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A silent letter is written but not said, like the k in knee or the b in lamb; most of them are fossils of sounds people really said hundreds of years ago, before the sounds wore away and the spelling stayed.
explain: In the Middle Ages "knight" was said with a k at the front and a throaty gh in the middle, like the ch in Scottish "loch"; German still says the k in Knie, its word for knee.
explain: A few silent letters were never spoken at all: scholars in the 1500s pushed an s into island and a b into debt and doubt to make them look like Latin or French words.
explain: The letters -ough are the champions of chaos: though, through, tough, cough, bough and thought all say -ough differently, because old sounds changed in different ways in different words.
explain: Names keep their spelling while speech shortens them: Wednesday (from Woden's day) has a silent d, and Worcestershire is said "WUSS-ter-sher"; for place names, listen to the locals.
fact: A silent letter is a letter you write but don't say.
fact: Middle English is English from about 1100 to 1500; in it the k in knight was said, and gh was a throaty sound like the ch in Scottish "loch".
fact: In Old English, knee was cnēow and was said with a k; people stopped saying the k at the start of words around the 1600s; German Knie still has a spoken k.
fact: Island came from Old English "īegland" with no s; in the 1500s people wrongly linked it to French "isle" and Latin "insula" and added an s that was never spoken.
fact: Debt came from French "dette"; scholars added a b to show its Latin family (debitum), and doubt got its b the same way; the b was never spoken in English.
fact: -ough has several sounds: though (like go), through (like true), tough (like stuff), cough (like off), bough (like cow), thought (like taught).
fact: A bough is a big branch of a tree.
fact: Wednesday comes from "Woden's day", after the old Germanic god Woden, the same god the Norse called Odin.
fact: In lamb, climb, thumb, comb and bomb, the b after m is silent.
fact: The w in write, wrong, wrist and wreck was said in Old English and dropped away before r; in water it stands before a vowel, so it stayed.
fact: Worcestershire is said "WUSS-ter-sher", Leicester "LES-ter" and Gloucester "GLOS-ter".
fact: Colonel is said "kernel": it was first said from French "coronel" with an r, and the spelling was later changed to look like Italian "colonnello".
fact: "Ghoti" is a famous joke spelling of fish (gh as in tough, o as in women, ti as in nation); real English never uses those letters that way at the start or end of a word, so it is a joke, not a rule.
exercise: Why does knight have a k and a gh? | They were once spoken, and the spelling stayed after the sounds went | They were added later on purpose to make spelling harder for children to learn | They were put in by mistake by printers | They show the word is French
exercise: What sound did gh make in Middle English? | A throaty sound like ch in "loch" | The sound f, as in the word tough today | No sound at all | The sound g, as in goat
exercise: Which language still says the k in its word for knee? | German (Knie) | French (genou) | Italian | Spanish
exercise: Why does island have an s? | People wrongly linked it to "isle" and added it | Its s was always spoken in Old English, and later went quiet | It is short for "is land" | Printers ran out of other letters
exercise: Was the s in island ever spoken? | No, never | Yes, until the 1900s | Yes, in Scotland only | Only by kings
exercise: Freya says debt should be "det". Where did its b come from? | Scholars added it to show its Latin family | It was spoken out loud in Old English times | It came from the word "bet" | It shows the word is German
exercise: Which pair rhyme? | through and true | though and cough | tough and bough | cough and thought
exercise: What is a bough? | A big branch of a tree | A kind of bow and arrow | A type of boat | A bend in a river
exercise: Which -ough word rhymes with cow? | bough | though | tough | cough
exercise: Why does Wednesday have a silent d? | It comes from Woden's day, and the d stopped being said | The d was added to make the word look longer and grander | It is a French word | It was spelled wrong in an old calendar
exercise: Daisy writes "lam" for a baby sheep. What's the full spelling? | lamb | lamm | lambe | lahm
exercise: Which group all have a silent b? | climb, thumb, comb | climber, bomb, tub | thumb, number, cab | comb, rob, crumb
exercise: Why is the w silent in write but not in water? | It dropped before r, but stayed before a vowel | Water is a much newer word than write, so it kept its w sound | Write is a French word | Both are silent in Scotland
exercise: How do locals say Leicester? | LES-ter | LIE-ses-ter | LEI-chester | LEE-sester
exercise: Grace's dad says colonel like "kernel". Why? | It was said from French "coronel" and later spelled like Italian | It is a spelling mistake that everyone copies from old army books | Colonels used to grow corn | It is American, the British say "col-o-nel"
exercise: Mia's joke book says "ghoti" spells fish. Is that how English works? | No, it's a joke; those letters never sound like that in those places | Yes, ghoti is the old spelling of fish from the Middle Ages, and kids still learn it | Yes, in Scotland | No, ghoti spells "goat"
exercise: How many silent letters are in "knight"? | Three: k, g, h | One: k | Two: g and h | None
exercise: Which word has a silent letter that was never spoken? | debt | knee | knight | write
test: Your mate goes "silent letters were invented just to annoy kids, no cap." What's the truth? | Most are old sounds that wore away, and a few were added to look like Latin | He's right, they were all added just for school spelling tests | They were all printing mistakes | They are spoken in posh accents
test: A Scottish gran says "It's dreich, the loch's all grey." What sound does "ch" in loch make? | A throaty sound, like old English gh | A k sound, just like in the word "lock" | A sh sound, like "losh" | No sound at all
test: Your cousin reads "though, through, tough" out loud as "thuff, thruff, tuff". Which is right? | Only "tuff" is right | All three are right | None of them | Only "thruff"
test: What would happen if English spelling followed speech exactly? | Knee would be "nee" and lamb "lam" | Nothing, because English already spells every word as it sounds | All words would get longer | Only names would change
test: A Gen Alpha kid says "Wednesday's d is skibidi random." ("Skibidi" is a word kids shout that means nothing here.) What's the truth? | It comes from Woden's day, an old god's name | It's random, nobody knows why the d is there at all | It was added in 2010 | It's there to make it rhyme with Monday
test: An American tourist says "Wor-ces-ter-shire" letter by letter. A local laughs. How do locals say it? | WUSS-ter-sher | WOR-sester-shire | War-chester-shire | WOR-kes-ter-shire
test: Your brother says "every silent letter was once spoken." Which word proves him wrong? | island | knight | knee | write
test: In a cartoon, a knight says "Ka-nig-ht" with every letter. Which is true? | Long ago people did say the k and the gh, though not exactly like that | Nobody has ever said the k in knight, not even a thousand years ago | It was always said "nite" | It's said that way in America today
test: A boomer grandad from Cornwall, proper gurt keen on puzzles ("gurt" means very), asks: how many of "knee, wrist, lamb, water, bough" have a silent letter? | Four: knee, wrist, lamb, bough | Three: knee, wrist, lamb | Two: knee and lamb | Five: all of them
test: Your mate says doubt is spelt "doute" in very old books. Could that be true? | Yes, the b was added later by scholars | No, the b was always there and always spoken out loud | No, doubt is a new word | Yes, but only in America
test: Puzzle: which -ough word rhymes with "off"? | cough | though | bough | through
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the majority of silent letters in English? | They are vestiges of sounds formerly pronounced, retained in spelling after the sounds were lost | They were introduced in order to render instruction more difficult | They are errors of contemporary printers | They are pronounced in all formal registers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the letter "s" appears in the word "island". | Scholars of the sixteenth century erroneously associated the word with "isle" and "insula" | The letter was pronounced throughout the Old English period | The word is a compound of "is" and "land" | The letter denotes the plural number
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the word "Wednesday". | It derives from "Woden's day", the silent "d" reflecting its origin | It derives from a French word meaning the middle of the week | Its spelling is the result of a recent error | The "d" is pronounced in standard British English
exam: Ascertain which of the following words rhymes with "cow". | bough | though | tough | cough
